description:
Follow-up from review on PR #51 (wpass-aio).

The current shape - withBytes and retainAcross sharing the same backing buffer on DatabaseKey - has two type-level weaknesses the review flagged:

1. Two methods on one buffer: a future caller calling withBytes (zeros) then retainAcross (hands zeros to SQLCipher) would silently re-introduce wpass-aio's symptom. Nothing in the type system prevents this; nothing tracks 'already consumed.'
2. Awkward call site: retainAcross's (ByteArray) -> Unit signature forces the var openedDb / !! dance in SqlCipherDatabaseFactory because the lambda actually wants to return a value.

Proposed refactor (from the review, with the master-zeroing amendment so we don't regress security):

  public class RetainedKeyBuffer internal constructor(internal val bytes: ByteArray) : AutoCloseable {
      override fun close() { bytes.fill(0) }
  }

  public class DatabaseKey(private val bytes: ByteArray) {
      // withBytes unchanged.
      public fun copyForRetainedConsumer(): RetainedKeyBuffer {
          val copy = bytes.copyOf()
          bytes.fill(0)   // master zeroed once handed off; no leak
          return RetainedKeyBuffer(copy)
      }
  }

Factory becomes:

  val keyBuf = databaseKey.copyForRetainedConsumer()
  val db = try {
      SQLiteDatabase.openOrCreateDatabase(dbFile, keyBuf.bytes, null, null)
  } catch (t: Throwable) { keyBuf.close(); throw t }

Wins: no exception-skips-zeroing path needing comment-load-bearing try/catch, no shared-buffer footgun, no var/!! dance, OpenedDatabase still owns a RetainedKeyBuffer with the same close-ordering invariant.

Cost: one extra 32-byte heap allocation per DB open (negligible).

Not a blocker for wpass-aio shipping - the original symptom is fixed and the exception-skips-zero gap was patched in the same PR. This is hardening for type-level enforcement of the lifetime contract.
